{"v":1,"st":"CO","y":"2020","L":{"cd":{"f":"2020-cd.json","n":7,"lbl":"U.S. House","src":[["U.S. Census Bureau, cartographic boundary file cb_2020_us_cd116_500k","https://www2.census.gov/geo/tiger/GENZ2020/shp/cb_2020_us_cd116_500k.zip","35113ca7612b14333bfbf24ec074d01278bc8ea13d836213b1c7390ba945c830"]],"vint":"The Census Bureau's lines of the 116th Congress's districts (its file says session 116). The Bureau published no file of the 117th Congress's districts, the Congress the 2020 election chose.","chk":[7,7,0],"why":"","rec":["Court-adopted 'Moreno/South Map' (Denver District Court, order of 2011-11-10, after the General Assembly failed to pass a plan); seven districts","2012-06-26","Colorado Supreme Court, Hall v. Moreno, 2012 CO 14 (2012-02-27), No. 11SC842: the Denver District Court adopted the 'Moreno/South Map'; order and mandate affirming it issued 2011-12-05","https://www.coloradojudicial.gov/sites/default/files/2024-05/11SC842.pdf","Colorado Supreme Court affirmed by order and mandate 2011-12-05 and by opinion 2012-02-27 (Hall v. Moreno, 2012 CO 14). Used 2012 through 2020. Census file: the Bureau's user note says its products hold the 116th Congress and the 2018 election-year legislative lines and none of the later changes; the cached cb_2020 files carry CDSESSN 116 / LSY 2018. Cached files: cb_2020 cd116 has 7 Colorado districts, cb_2022 and cb_2024 have 8, and none of the 7 matches."],"c":13,"lv":{"federal":7,"state":6}},"sldu":{"f":"2020-sldu.json","n":35,"lbl":"State Senate","src":[["U.S. Census Bureau, cartographic boundary file cb_2020_us_sldu_500k","https://www2.census.gov/geo/tiger/GENZ2020/shp/cb_2020_us_sldu_500k.zip","5fcedd3ac126a22d9beaefda04abf217f27bc29e1f515a7255b1c3b6c13bb594"]],"vint":"The Census Bureau's 2020 file of legislative districts, the lines it records for the 2018 legislative session year. The Bureau published no file of the lines in use for 2020. Where a state's lines changed in between, the check below or the plan cited stops the map.","chk":[18,18,0],"why":"","rec":["Colorado Reapportionment Commission 2011 plan (revised plan adopted 2011-11-29, approved by the Colorado Supreme Court in December 2011); 35 Senate districts","2012-06-26","Colorado Supreme Court, In re Reapportionment of the Colorado General Assembly, No. 11SA282 (2011-11-15): first Commission plan disapproved, to be resubmitted by 2011-12-06","https://www.coloradojudicial.gov/sites/default/files/2024-05/11SA282.pdf","The Commission's first plan (adopted 2011-09-19) was disapproved by the Supreme Court on 2011-11-15 for ignoring county boundaries; the revised plan replaced it. Approval date: 2011-12-12 per a county clerk's page; I did not find the court's own order. The Bureau's 2018 page lists Colorado among states that 'delineated new' legislative boundaries for 2018; I found no new Colorado plan after 2011, so I read that as a re-delineation of the same plan, and the cb_2020 file carries those 2018 lines (LSY 2018). Census file: the Bureau's user note says its products hold the 116th Congress and the 2018 election-year legislative lines and none of the later changes; the cached cb_2020 files carry CDSESSN 116 / LSY 2018."],"c":18,"lv":{"legislature":18}},"sldl":{"f":"2020-sldl.json","n":65,"lbl":"State House","src":[["U.S. Census Bureau, cartographic boundary file cb_2020_us_sldl_500k","https://www2.census.gov/geo/tiger/GENZ2020/shp/cb_2020_us_sldl_500k.zip","cac23d32db3b74bb50ca5e02e085cf75dcd4ec20f754aad5bea3aae0a3985031"]],"vint":"The Census Bureau's 2020 file of legislative districts, the lines it records for the 2018 legislative session year. The Bureau published no file of the lines in use for 2020. Where a state's lines changed in between, the check below or the plan cited stops the map.","chk":[65,65,0],"why":"","rec":["Colorado Reapportionment Commission 2011 plan (revised plan adopted 2011-11-29, approved by the Colorado Supreme Court in December 2011); 65 House districts","2012-06-26","Colorado Supreme Court, In re Reapportionment of the Colorado General Assembly, No. 11SA282 (2011-11-15): first Commission plan disapproved, to be resubmitted by 2011-12-06","https://www.coloradojudicial.gov/sites/default/files/2024-05/11SA282.pdf","Same Commission order and approval as the Senate plan. The Bureau's 2018 page lists Colorado among states that 'delineated new' legislative boundaries for 2018; I found no new Colorado plan after 2011, so I read that as a re-delineation of the same plan, and the cb_2020 file carries those 2018 lines (LSY 2018). Census file: the Bureau's user note says its products hold the 116th Congress and the 2018 election-year legislative lines and none of the later changes; the cached cb_2020 files carry CDSESSN 116 / LSY 2018."],"c":65,"lv":{"legislature":65}},"place":{"f":"2020-place.json","n":68,"lbl":"Cities, towns and villages","src":[["U.S. Census Bureau, cartographic boundary file cb_2020_us_place_500k","https://www2.census.gov/geo/tiger/GENZ2020/shp/cb_2020_us_place_500k.zip","0d177eb93798cfbc88cdcbafed401dc0a3e6d9b062f80a3d431d4f903a0fc0e4"]],"vint":"The Census Bureau's 2020 file (boundaries as of January 1, 2020).","chk":[],"why":"","c":121,"lv":{"city":33,"measure":88}},"sd":{"f":"2020-sd.json","n":23,"lbl":"School districts","src":[["U.S. Census Bureau, cartographic boundary file cb_2020_us_unsd_500k","https://www2.census.gov/geo/tiger/GENZ2020/shp/cb_2020_us_unsd_500k.zip","74c876b9663129e9c6f1aff01a5ba716e389f9b476ba4f8184c5bccb627f6fa5"]],"vint":"The Census Bureau's 2020 file (boundaries as of January 1, 2020).","chk":[],"why":"","c":25,"lv":{"measure":25}}},"t":{"state":{"CO":["president","us-senate","amendment-76","amendment-77","amendment-b","amendment-c","proposition-113","proposition-114","proposition-115","proposition-116","proposition-117","proposition-118","proposition-ee","retention-state-court-melissa-hart","retention-state-court-carlos-a-samour-jr","retention-state-court-ted-c-tow-iii","retention-state-court-craig-r-welling"]}